  { q: 'What is the "beast of battle" formula in Norse poetry?', a: 'The "beasts of battle" is a traditional poetic formula found in Old Norse, Old English (Anglo-Saxon), Old High German, and other Germanic poetry. It describes the wolf, the raven, and the eagle — creatures that follow armies and feed on the slain. In Old Norse poetry the formula appears in various battle descriptions: before a great fight, ravens croak, eagles scream, and wolves howl in anticipation. This conventional motif reflects real observation of these scavengers following armed conflict.' },
