    <h1>Ravens in Skaldic Poetry: Kennings, Battle Birds, and Odin's Flock</h1>
  </header>

  <section class="qa-snippet" aria-label="Direct answer">
    <p><strong class="claim-lore">[lore]</strong> Ravens are among the most frequent images in Old Norse skaldic poetry. As battle scavengers, they form the core of the "beasts of battle" formula — wolf, raven, and eagle croaking and circling over the slain. As kenning elements, the raven generates dozens of compound metaphors for swords, battles, wounds, and death. As Odin's birds, they appear in Odinic poetry representing divine knowledge and the battlefield deaths that feed both god and bird.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>Skaldic Poetry and the Raven</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-lore">[lore]</strong> Old Norse skaldic poetry is a highly formal, technically demanding court poetry using complex metres (<em>dróttkvætt</em> and others) and elaborate periphrastic compounds called kennings. It was composed by named poets (skalds) from the 9th to 13th centuries and preserved in later saga texts and the poetry manuscripts. The raven appears in this tradition as:<sup><a href="#src-1">1</a></sup></p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>A battle-bird:</strong> in almost every military poem, ravens attend the battlefield to eat the dead. This is not mere metaphor — ravens genuinely follow armies and feed on casualties, and the skalds drew on this observable fact.</li>
      <li><strong>A kenning element:</strong> kennings are periphrastic two-part compounds. The raven generates a whole cluster of kenning types — for battles, wounds, swords, and warriors.</li>
      <li><strong>An Odinic symbol:</strong> in poems touching on Odin and his attributes, ravens appear as the god's familiars, embodying his supernatural knowledge.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>Raven Kennings</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-lore">[lore]</strong> Some representative kenning types using ravens from the skaldic corpus:<sup><a href="#src-1">1</a><sup><a href="#src-2">2</a></sup></p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Kennings for "battle":</strong> <em>hrafnseðja</em> ("raven-feast," "food for ravens"), <em>hrafnleikr</em> ("raven-play") — battles are events that provide feasts for ravens.</li>
      <li><strong>Kennings for "sword":</strong> swords are often described as the implements that create raven-feasts — the sword is the bird's "feeder" or "nourisher."</li>
      <li><strong>Kennings for "warrior" or "lord":</strong> a generous lord who provides great battles is a "feeder of ravens" or "raven-gladdener."</li>
      <li><strong>Kennings for "wound":</strong> wounds are described via the raven — "raven's drink" (blood), "raven's red" (blood).</li>
      <li><strong>Kennings for "corpse":</strong> the dead body is the raven's food — "raven-prey," "raven-portion."</li>
    </ul>
    <p>This cluster of kenning types shows how central the raven was to the Old Norse poetic imagination of violence, war, and death.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>The Beasts of Battle</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-lore">[lore]</strong> Old Norse poetry shares with Old English poetry a "beasts of battle" formula: before or during a great battle, three scavengers anticipate the feast of the slain — the wolf (<em>úlfr</em>), the raven (<em>hrafn</em>), and the eagle (<em>örn</em>). These three constitute a conventional triad in both traditions, reflecting real behavioural ecology (wolves, ravens, and eagles genuinely co-occur at large carrion events) elevated into a standard poetic formula.<sup><a href="#src-2">2</a></sup></p>
    <p>The formula appears in Old English poems including Beowulf, Exodus, and The Battle of Brunanburh; in Old Norse it recurs across saga verse, Eddic poems, and lausavísur. Its shared presence across both traditions suggests Proto-Germanic origins or at least very early parallel development.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>The Raven and Odin in Skaldic Verse</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-lore">[lore]</strong> In skaldic poems touching on Odin, the raven figures as his attribute. The god is called <em>Hrafnáss</em> ("Raven-god") and <em>Hrafnaguð</em> ("Raven-deity") in several kennings for Odin. The connection between the valraven (the raven of the slain) and Odin (the god who chooses the slain — the Allfather) is built into the fabric of skaldic poetry: the ravens eating on the battlefield are implicitly Odin's ravens gathering information and nourishment for the god from the sacrifice of warriors.<sup><a href="#src-1">1</a></sup></p>
  </section>

